        "like": 3
      },
      "sourceLink": null,
      "spam": false,
      "statusChangeID": null,
      "value": "I'm going to go against the general view here and say that I think the limit could be increased, but not entirely removed. Contrary to what many believe, polycount alone isn't that much of a lag factor for most modern GPUs even with materials and lighting until it hits an insane number like 1.5M+. Polys are easy - They're one of the most primitive draw operations in every renderer and take a matter of nanoseconds to complete. Most singleplayer modern games have character-model meshes with up to 250K polys as an average, so I don't think it's much of an issue if VRC increases the limit to say 100K, to accommodate for avatar count in a world. I think their current limit is fairly generous, however, as the amount of polys in a world - especially worlds with high player count - can add up and cause performance issues.\n\n\nI used to have trouble with this but I realized something in VRC: Most people aren't going to be looking at your model like it's some 4K render you've done. If you've got a barrel of a gun that's just a cylinder with say, 40 sides on it? It's not that big a deal unless the barrel is really huge. You can cut polycount on smaller objects and allocate the numbers to the larger stuff. Even if the small stuff is a detail people will be looking at, it's harder to see jagged edges on a small object in a game unless you put your face right up against it.",
      "viewerReactions": null
    },
